// minipage-demo — a minipage typesets a block of text as paragraphs in a box of a fixed width, so a self-contained
// passage can sit in the running text as a single rigid box.
//
//     \begin{minipage}[pos]{width} ... \end{minipage}      // the environment, over the engine's \beginminipage pair
//     \parbox[pos]{width}{ ... }                            // the command form, same machinery
//
// The optional [pos] aligns the box on the surrounding baseline — t (first line), b (last line), c (centre, default).
// Because a minipage is one atomic box, it is the natural payload for a wrapped figure: the running text flows around
// the whole block as a unit and it never splits across the column. A minipage is rectangular, so pair it with a plain
// (rectangular) \wrapfigure — a shaped wrap is for a figure whose drawn outline is the shape, not for a box.

\use{document}
\use{float}

\title {Minipages}
\author {a block of text as a box}
\maketitle

\section{A minipage on its own}

A minipage holds one or more paragraphs, line-broken to its own width, and reports a single box to the surrounding
text. Framed here to show its extent:

\noindent\fbox{\begin{minipage}{2.6in}
This minipage is 2.6 inches wide. Its body is set as ordinary paragraphs, justified to that width, with the
paragraph machinery working exactly as it does on the page.

A second paragraph indents on its first line, just like body text, proving the block is a little page of its own
rather than a single run of text.
\end{minipage}}

\section{Two minipages side by side}

Set side by side, with stretchable glue between them — the classic two-column aside — each takes its share of the
line and aligns at the top:

\noindent\begin{minipage}[t]{2.5in}
\textbf{On the left.} Minipages placed next to each other on one line make a simple multi-column layout without any
page-level column machinery. Each is independent and as tall as its own content.
\end{minipage}\hfill\begin{minipage}[t]{2.5in}
\textbf{On the right.} The taller of the two sets the height of the line; a [t] alignment keeps their first lines
level, which is what you almost always want for paired captions or notes.
\end{minipage}

\section{A minipage wrapped by running text}

\wrapfigure{r}{2.3in}{
  \fbox{\begin{minipage}{2.1in}
\textbf{A boxed aside.} Because the whole block is one box, the running text flows around it as a unit, and the box
keeps its own internal paragraph breaks.

It will never split across a page or column the way loose wrapped text can.
\end{minipage}}
}
